# ignore until Vue2 to Vue3 upgrade
CVE-2025-15284

# brace-expansion + ip-address vulnerabilities vendored *inside npm itself*
# (usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules), installed by
# `npm i -g npm@latest` in the Dockerfile. Not from td.server/td.vue's own
# dependency trees - those are patched as of the ip-address/brace-expansion
# security bumps merged 2026-08-07. As of npm 12.0.2, the vendored copies are:
#   brace-expansion 5.0.7 (needs 5.0.8 for CVE-2026-14257, 5.0.9 for CVE-2026-69152)
#   ip-address      10.2.0 (needs 10.3.1 for CVE-2026-69192)
# Since these come from `npm@latest`, they can only be resolved upstream by
# npm shipping a newer release with bumped vendored deps - not by anything
# in this repo's package.json/package-lock.json. Re-check by running the
# Dockerfile's `npm i -g npm@latest` step and scanning the result with trivy.
# (A related, now-fixed CVE-2026-13149 was removed from this file: verified
# via the same method that npm 12.0.2's vendored brace-expansion 5.0.7
# already satisfies its fix version.)
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152
CVE-2026-69192

# docs/ contains fake example credentials (BITBUCKET_CLIENT_ID/SECRET =
# 'deadbeef...') used purely as documentation placeholders in
# docs/configure/*.md and the generated Jekyll site (docs/configure/*.html,
# docs/assets/search.json). Trivy's secret scanner flags these as real
# Bitbucket client IDs. Scoped to docs/ only and to this specific rule so a
# real secret elsewhere in the image would still be caught.
bitbucket-client-id docs/*
